 ```
 
+## Creating a JSON Patch
+
+`createPatch(from, to)` (from `src/index.ts`) produces a JSON Patch, as RFC 6902, that turns the document `from` into the document `to`
+when it is applied with `applyPatch`. It exists so that a client can send only what changed between two versions of a document, instead
+of the whole new version. The patch describes the changes and nothing more: equal members and elements produce no operation, a changed
+member is replaced at its own pointer, and an inserted or removed array element is one `add` or `remove`, so the elements after it are
+not sent again.
+
+It returns the patch as a new array and never modifies its inputs; the values in the patch are copies. It throws a `JsonPatchError`
+when either document is not a JSON value. Two documents that differ in one small place produce a patch of that one operation, but the
+patch is not always the smallest possible: for an array whose changed middle is very large, the elements are paired by position.
+
+```ts
+createPatch({ foo: ["bar", "baz"] }, { foo: ["bar", "qux"] }); // [{ op: "replace", path: "/foo/1", value: "qux" }]
+applyPatch({ foo: ["bar", "baz"] }, createPatch({ foo: ["bar", "baz"] }, { foo: ["bar", "qux"] })); // { foo: ["bar", "qux"] }
+```
+
 ## JSON Merge Patch
 
 `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` (from `src/index.ts`) applies a JSON Merge Patch, as [RFC 7386](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7386),
